※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ A function is pure unless it says otherwise, and what pure means is that
※ calling it changes nothing a later call or a later reader could notice.  That
※ is what lets a caller move a call, make it once instead of twice, or not make
※ it at all -- which is worth having by default rather than on request.
※
※ A pure function may read anything, including a variable at the top level; it
※ may write its own storage, an array it made being gone when the call ends; and
※ it may call other pure functions.  What it may not do is change anything that
※ was there before the call or is there after it.

let base: u8 = 20u8

fn doubled(n: u8) → u8:
    n + n

fn summed(v: u8⟦3⟧) → u8:
    ※ reading an array it was handed is not changing it
    v⟦0⟧ + v⟦1⟧ + v⟦2⟧

fn worked_out() → u8:
    ※ its own storage, written and then read, all inside one call
    let room: mut u8⟦3⟧ = ⟦1u8, 0u8, 0u8⟧
    room⟦1⟧ ← 0u8
    ※ reading a variable at the top level, and calling another pure function
    summed(room) + doubled(base)

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    worked_out() + 1u8
